  "worked_solution": "First, we convert the inequality into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y & >-5 \\\\ -y & >-x-5 \\\\ y & <x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed as usual. We start by drawing the corresponding straight line $y=x+5.$ Since the inequality involves a 'strictly less than' sign, the line $y=x+5$ itself is *not* part of the solution. Therefore, we draw a dashed line.\n\nThen, we need to decide whether to shade above or below the line. The inequality $y<x+5$ refers to the $y$-values that are *less than* those on the line $y=x+5,$ so we shade *below* the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996642/assets/bd0ce52fe5aaf5e95c4f2eb5bd14652ee696c7bb2dfdb99ccdf07543be46f45e.png)",
